notes:
Drops the LM-1 CPU entirely: the 43-block token CFG from snake-finish compiled
through lllm_layout's now-generic planner. 1.69e9 -> 3.82e8, a 4.4x.
Server ticks ran 1.51x local (22,932 vs 15,147).

Three bugs the snake-finish branch left, none of which showed as a load error:
 * FLAT_RELAY had never run — its returning man walked into the south wall and
   its spawn sat where a turn was needed; `@` is only a nop.
 * The two "proven" halves were proven against DIFFERENT protocols: WORKER emits
   (addr, colour) pairs closed by a negative, painter() expected a leading count.
   Each had passed its own verification. The worker cannot know the count — a
   move's second colour waits on the collision scan and a death repaints a body
   of unknown length — so the painter was rebuilt around the terminator.
 * The router checked cells at draw time only, so a later edge could drop a `v`
   inside an earlier edge's vertical and hijack that man.

Measurement worth keeping: ordering blocks by a PROFILE of the public cases
scored better on paper and measured WORSE (17.7k vs 15.8k ticks) — weighting
folds the cold death paths into the hot loop. The objective is unweighted.

Why not rank 1 (needs 3.42e7): pipe binding is nearest-Manhattan within one room,
so the RING/IO split is a single linear order in x, and two side-by-side block
banks would need the same pipe both west and east of the code. That forces one
block per row band (~2 rows straight, ~3-4 per branch), so 44 blocks -> 118 rows.
Height binds at 129 against width 70. Reaching rank 1 needs real 2D packing where
blocks share rows with each other's corridors — a different placer.
